{
  "question": "Does each external factor improve out-of-sample prediction of sales beyond a transactions-only baseline?",
  "protocol": {
    "unit": "branch x day",
    "baseline": "Poisson GLM: branch + branch x weekday + branch x linear trend (ridge 1e-4)",
    "directions": [
      "Jan-Mar -> Apr-Jun",
      "Apr-Jun -> Jan-Mar"
    ],
    "split_date": "2026-04-01",
    "metric": "relative reduction in held-out mean Poisson deviance",
    "placebo": "circular shifts of the factor by 14-167 days (154 offsets)",
    "pass_rule": "gain > 0 in both directions and Holm-adjusted max(p_fwd, p_rev) < 0.05",
    "positive_control": "binomial thinning on flagged days, 100 simulations per cell",
    "raw_lines": "all retained; orders counted as distinct Transaction IDs"
  },
  "limits": [
    "Weather is realised weather (perfect-knowledge retrospective test), not a forecast-time input.",
    "Conflict and strike dates were selected after the fact; branch exposure is unverified.",
    "Branch locations are unverified; weather is tested at three candidate locations.",
    "Passing shows predictive association, not causation; failing does not prove zero effect (see positive_control for detectable sizes).",
    "These factors are not inputs to the strict model (src/strict_model.py).",
    "Event-list factors whose flagged days all fall in one half cannot pass by construction: the factor is all zeros in either the training or the test half, so the factor cannot be scored on unseen flagged days. A non-zero gain for such a factor only reflects how removing its training days changes the baseline fit (for example the March 29 and Eid rows), not prediction. Their rows record that the test cannot see them, not that the event had no effect (see notebooks/09_event_checks.ipynb for the in-sample screen with a false-discovery correction)."
  ],
